NAME
       neo - text editor

SYNOPSIS
       neo [file]

DESCRIPTION
       A full-screen editor in the style of nano: the text fills the
       screen, the cursor sits where you type, and the keys are shown
       at the bottom.

KEYS
       Ctrl-S   save
       Ctrl-Q   exit, twice if there are unsaved changes
       Esc      exit as well
       Ctrl-F   search
       Ctrl-G   go to a line number
       Ctrl-X   cut the current line
       Ctrl-C   copy the current line
       Ctrl-V   paste
       Ctrl-D   duplicate the current line
       Ctrl-B   jump to the start of the file
       Ctrl-E   jump to the end of the file
       Ctrl-N   line numbers on or off
       Ctrl-L   pick the language for colouring
       Ctrl-T   file tree
       Ctrl-P   settings
       arrows, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End   move around

SETTINGS
       Ctrl-P opens the settings. Left and right change a value, Enter
       acts on a line that says <Enter>.

         Auto-pairs       type ( and get ), and the same for quotes
         Line numbers     a number column down the left edge
         Syntax           which language the colouring follows
         Tab width        how wide a tab is drawn, 1 to 16
         Quit confirm     ask before leaving an unsaved file
         Expand tabs      insert spaces instead of a tab character
         Auto indent      a new line starts where the last one did
         Trim on save     drop trailing whitespace when saving
         Highlight line   shade the line the cursor is on
         Show tabs        draw tabs as > and dots, to see them
         Backup on save   copy the old file to <name>.bak first
         Scroll margin    lines kept above and below the cursor, 0 to 20
         Edit syntax rules  the colouring editor, see below
         Save settings    write them to ~/.neorc

       ~/.neorc is read at startup, so the settings survive a reboot on
       an installed system.

FILES
       ~/.neorc

SEE ALSO
       cfm(1), less(1)

SYNTAX COLOURING
       C, JavaScript, CSS, HTML, assembly, Python, shell, Rust, Go,
       Lua, SQL, Markdown and configuration files are recognised by
       extension.

       To colour anything else, or to override one of those, write
       /etc/neo/<extension>.syn - a file called ml.syn colours every
       file ending .ml. One setting per line, name = value; blank lines
       and lines beginning # are ignored.

         name       what to call it in the status bar
         comment    what starts a comment running to end of line
         block      what opens and closes a block comment, two words
         keywords   words to colour as keywords; repeat to add more
         types      words to colour as type names, second colour
         strings    single if ' quotes as well as ", double if only "
         backtick   1 if backticks quote a string

       For example:

         name = Pascal
         comment = //
         block = (* *)
         keywords = begin end if then else while do procedure function
         types = integer real char boolean string

       /etc/neo/csh.syn ships as a working example.

       Writing the file by hand is optional: Edit syntax rules in the
       settings is a form for the same thing. It opens on the extension
       of the file being edited, fills in whatever rules already exist,
       and each line explains itself as you move onto it. Enter edits a
       field, Ctrl-S writes the file and recolours the text at once.
       Esc leaves without writing. When a Cervus system is mounted at
       /mnt the file is written to /mnt/etc/neo so it survives a reboot;
       otherwise it goes to /etc/neo, which a live session forgets.
